## Office and Retail Vacancies Weigh on REIT Earnings

Since the pandemic, surging office vacancies and retail structural shifts have left commercial real estate operators facing dual pressure: plummeting tenant occupancy and imminent high-rate refinancing of the massive mortgages issued during the low-rate era. As asset values fall, collateral values decline in tandem, deepening a structural crisis.

## Commercial Real Estate Operators Face Reality: Revenue Growth Turns Negative

**Realty Income** (**O**), one of the largest commercial real estate REITs in the United States, closed at $59.26 on November 12, with a market cap of $56.2B. The same day, industrial real estate specialist **Rexford Industrial** (**REXR**) closed at $37.64 with a market cap of $8.2B. The performance gap between the two companies reveals the depth of the crisis.

O maintains a net profit margin of 21.8% and revenue growth of 10.2% year-over-year, appearing relatively healthy. However, its P/E ratio of 40.4 is above historical averages, suggesting the market has already priced in significant future growth. By contrast, REXR's situation is far more severe. Its net profit margin stands at -47.1%, deep in the red, with revenue declining at -5.6% year-over-year. Industrial real estate was thought to be holding up relatively well, but this shows vacancies and rental pressure have penetrated even that segment.

The State Street SPDR S&P Retail ETF (**XRT**), which tracks retail, closed at $90.67 on November 12, recording gains of +7.05% over one month and +2.15% over three months. Despite the gains, its P/E ratio of 15.2 is below the broader market (approximately 18–20), indicating the entire retail sector trades at a discount in market sentiment.

## Why Are Real Estate Operators Shaking Now? Four Factors

**1. Structural Office Vacancies from the Pandemic**: Since 2020, the normalization of remote work has locked in office occupancy at depressed levels without recovery. As companies have downsized office footprints and sustained flexible work arrangements, high vacancy rates in expensive urban offices remain unresolved. With tenants shrinking and reluctant to renew leases, operators face persistent revenue pressure.

**2. Irreversible Retail Structural Shift**: E-commerce growth continues to force physical retail closures. As traditional malls and street retail lose tenants, landlords' negotiating power over rent weakens. Remaining tenants, facing operational headwinds, lack the capacity to absorb rent increases.

**3. Maturing Mortgages and High-Rate Refinancing**: Mortgages issued during the ultra-low-rate environment of the mid-2010s are coming due between 2024 and 2026. Capital borrowed at rock-bottom rates must now be refinanced at significantly higher current rates. With property values declining, collateral-based lending tightens, and refinancing rates exceed debt service capacity.

**4. Asset Depreciation Forces Loss Recognition**: Rising vacancy rates and declining rents force revaluation of property assets. As collateral values fall, existing loan covenants—particularly loan-to-value and debt ratios—risk violation, requiring capital injection or asset sales to realign. This signals deteriorating long-term cash flows.

## Why REITs May Still Endure: Structural Strengths vs. Weaknesses

**Strengths**: REITs are structurally designed around dividend-yielding income streams. O's 10.2% revenue growth and 21.8% net margin demonstrate continued capacity to sustain dividend payouts. Industrial real estate, REXR's focus, benefits from steady e-commerce logistics tenant demand. With market repricing already partially underway, downside room may be limited.

**Weaknesses**: High leverage and limited liquidity are fatal vulnerabilities. REXR's -47.1% net margin means interest costs and valuation losses exceed operating cash flow. Even O, with stronger assets, trades at P/E 40.4 in overvalued territory, facing significant downside risk if rates rise further or growth slows.

## Takeaway

The commercial real estate sector faces simultaneous shocks from structural change and financial environment shifts. High P/E ratios and negative net margins signal the market has already priced in the crisis, but refinancing pressure likely keeps downside momentum alive through 2027. Industrial real estate shows relative resilience, but office and retail face structurally challenging paths to recovery.

